.\" This page is maintained by hand.  It started out as help2man output, but
.\" it now covers the commands as well as the options, and regenerating it
.\" would throw most of it away.
.TH SQLRSH "1" "August 2026" "SQL Relay" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sqlrsh \- SQL Relay command line database shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sqlrsh
\fB\-host\fR host [\fB\-port\fR port] [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR]
.br
.B sqlrsh
\fB\-socket\fR socket [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR]
.br
.B sqlrsh
[\fB\-config\fR config] \fB\-id\fR id [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
sqlrsh is the SQL Relay command line database shell.
.PP
It can be used interactively, or non\-interactively to run queries directly
from the command line, or scripts containing queries.
.PP
At least one of \fB\-host\fR, \fB\-socket\fR or \fB\-id\fR has to be given.
Without one of them, sqlrsh writes a usage message and exits 1.
.PP
Interactively, sqlrsh writes a banner, then a prompt, and reads a query or a
command.  Non\-interactively, it runs the queries and commands in the string
given to \fB\-command\fR, the single query given to \fB\-query\fR, or in the
file named by \fB\-script\fR, and exits.  With none of those and \fB\-batch\fR
given, it reads queries and commands from standard input, the same as an
interactive session, but without the banner or the prompts.
.PP
Everything ends with the delimiter, which is a semicolon unless
\fB\-delimiter\fR or the delimiter command changes it.  A query may be split
over as many lines as it takes.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Connection options:"
.TP
\fB\-host\fR host
Host name or IP address of the server to
connect to.
.TP
\fB\-port\fR port
Port to connect to.
.TP
\fB\-socket\fR socket
Local unix socket file name to connect to.
Can be used instead of host/port for making
connections to local servers.
.TP
\fB\-user\fR user
User name to auth with.
.TP
\fB\-password\fR password
Password to auth with.
.SS "Alternate connection options:"
.TP
\fB\-config\fR config
Override the default configuration with the
specified configuration.
.TP
\fB\-id\fR instanceid
Derive connection info and credentials from the
specified instance, as defined in the
configuration.
.RS
.PP
If the instance's password is encrypted, sqlrsh decrypts it with the module
named by the user's passwordencryptionid.  That only works for a two-way
encryption.  A one-way encryption cannot decrypt anything, and a
passwordencryptionid that no passwordencryption in the configuration matches
has no module at all.  Either one is a usage error, reported by name, and
sqlrsh exits 1.  Use \fB\-user\fR and \fB\-password\fR along with \fB\-id\fR
in that case.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-krb\fR
Use Kerberos authentication and encryption.
.TP
\fB\-krbservice\fR svc
Use the specified kerberos service.
.TP
\fB\-krbmech\fR mech
Use the specified kerberos mechanism.
.TP
\fB\-krbflags\fR flags
Use the specified kerberos flags,
comma\-separated.
.TP
\fB\-tls\fR
Use TLS/SSL authentication and encryption.
.TP
\fB\-tlsversion\fR version
Use the specified TLS/SSL version.
.TP
\fB\-tlscert\fR file
Use the specified certificate chain file.
This file should contain the client's
certificate, private key, and signing
certificates, as appropriate.
On Windows systems, this must be a .pfx file.
On non\-Windows systems, a variety of file
formats are supported.
.TP
\fB\-tlspassword\fR pwd
Use the specified password to access the private
key in the file specified by \fB\-tlscert\fR.
.TP
\fB\-tlsciphers\fR "list"
Allow the specified list of ciphers.  The
list should be quoted and the ciphers should be
separated by spaces.
.TP
\fB\-tlsvalidate\fR (no|ca|ca+host|ca+domain)
Certificate validation option.
"no" \- Don't validate the server's certificate.
"ca" \- Validate that the server's certificate
was signed by a trusted certificate authority.
"ca+host" \- Perform "ca" validation and also
validate that one of the subject alternate names
(or common name if no SANs are present) in the
certificate matches the host parameter.
(Falls back to "ca" validation when a unix
socket is used.)
"ca+domain" \- Perform "ca" validation and also
validate that the domain name of one of the
subject alternate names (or common name if no
SANs are present) in the certificate matches
the domain name of the host parameter.
(Falls back to "ca" validation when a unix
socket is used.)
.TP
\fB\-tlsca\fR file
Use the specified certificate authority file
when validating the server's certificate.  Or,
if "file" is a directory, then use all
certificate authority files found in that
directory when validating the server's
certificate.
.TP
\fB\-tlsdepth\fR depth
Set the maximum certificate chain validation
depth to the specified depth.
.SS "Presets:"
.TP
\fB\-agent\fR
Shorthand for \fB\-quiet\fR \fB\-format\fR jsonl
\fB\-nullsasnulls\fR on \fB\-continueonerror\fR on: the
settings a script or an agent wants for output that's
quiet, one json object per line, nulls distinguishable
from empty strings, and a run that keeps going past a
failed statement.  Any of those given explicitly, such
as \fB\-format\fR csv, still wins.
.SS "Command options:"
.TP
\fB\-script\fR filename
Run the specified script which contains commands
or queries that could otherwise be run at the
sqlrsh prompt.
.TP
\fB\-command\fR "commands"
Run the provided string which contains commands
or queries that could otherwise be run at the
sqlrsh prompt.
.TP
\fB\-query\fR "query"
Run the provided string as a single SQL statement.
Unlike \fB\-command\fR, the string is never scanned
for the delimiter, so an embedded, unquoted
delimiter character can't split or truncate it.
A trailing delimiter, if present, is trimmed along
with trailing whitespace, but neither is required.
.TP
\fB\-batch\fR
With none of \fB\-script\fR, \fB\-command\fR or
\fB\-query\fR given, read and run commands from
standard input, the same as an interactive session,
but without the banner or the prompts, so piped\-in
SQL doesn't come back mixed in with the output.
Unlike a real interactive session, a failed
statement counts, and the exit code reflects it,
the same as \fB\-script\fR and \fB\-command\fR.  See
EXIT STATUS and CONTINUING PAST AN ERROR below.
.TP
\fB\-delimiter\fR char
End each command or query with the specified
character.  Defaults to a semicolon.
\fB\-delimeter\fR is accepted as a spelling of it.
Unlike the delimiter command, this option does
not echo the character back.
.TP
\fB\-continueonerror\fR on|off
Carry on past a statement that failed, rather
than stopping at the first one.  Defaults to
off.  See CONTINUING PAST AN ERROR below.
.SS "Output options:"
.TP
\fB\-format\fR plain|csv|json|jsonl
Format the output as specified.
Defaults to plain.  See OUTPUT FORMATS below.
.TP
\fB\-quiet\fR on|off
Shorthand for \fB\-headers\fR off \fB\-stats\fR off.
Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-headers\fR on|off
Write the column names above the result set.
plain format only.  Defaults to on.
.TP
\fB\-divider\fR on|off
Write a line of = signs under the column names.
plain format only.  Defaults to on.
.TP
\fB\-stats\fR on|off
Write the statistics after the result set.
plain writes a block of labels, json and jsonl
carry the same numbers inside the document, and
csv has no statistics at all.  Defaults to on.
.TP
\fB\-noelapsed\fR on|off
Leave the elapsed time out of the statistics.
Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-fieldsas\fR raw|number|boolean|date
Fetch each field with the matching getFieldAs
method, where the column suits it.  Defaults to
raw, the field as the database sent it.  See
FETCHING FIELDS AS A TYPE below.
.TP
\fB\-getasnumber\fR on|off
Calls getFieldAs(Integer|Double) as appropriate.
An alias \- on is \fB\-fieldsas\fR number and off
is \fB\-fieldsas\fR raw.  Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-nullsasnulls\fR on|off
Get nulls as nulls, rather than as empty
strings.  csv, json and jsonl can only tell the
two apart while this is on.  Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-debug\fR on|off
Write the client library debug messages to
stdout.  Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-debug\fR filename
Write them to the named file instead.
.SS "Fetch options:"
.TP
\fB\-resultsetbuffersize\fR rows
Fetch result sets using the specified number of
rows at once.  0 means fetch the whole result
set at once.  Defaults to 100.
.TP
\fB\-lazyfetch\fR on|off
Fetch rows as they are asked for, rather than
when the query runs.  Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-nextresultset\fR on|off
Fetch every result set the query returns, rather
than just the first.  Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-txqueries\fR on|off
Send begin, commit and rollback to the database
as queries, rather than running them as
commands.  Defaults to off.
.SS "Bind variable options:"
.TP
\fB\-validatebinds\fR on|off
Ignore bind variables the query does not have,
rather than failing.  Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-bindvariabledelimiters\fR delimiters
Recognize the given bind variable delimiters,
from the set ? : @ $.
.SS "Session options:"
.TP
\fB\-final\fR on|off
End the session after every query, so each one
gets a session of its own.  Defaults to off.
.TP
\fB\-autocommit\fR on|off
Commit after every query.  Without this the
server decides, so there is no default here.
.SS "Other options:"
.TP
\fB\-locale\fR env|locale_name
Calls setlocale(LC_ALL, locale_name).
env means use LC variables.
.TP
\fB\-localstatedir\fR dir
Override the default directory for keeping
working or stateful files, the result set cache
among them, with the specified directory.
.TP
\fB\-help\fR
Write the help and exit.  Has to be the only
option on the command line.
.TP
\fB\-version\fR
Write the version and exit.  Has to be the only
option on the command line.
.SH "OPTION VALUES"
An option that takes a value has to be given one, so \fB\-resultsetbuffersize\fR
by itself exits 1.
.PP
A value that begins with a dash has to be given as \fB\-\-option\fR=value,
because "\fB\-option\fR \fB\-value\fR" reads the dash as the next option.
.SH "ON AND OFF"
An on|off option with no value means on, so \fB\-headers\fR by itself turns
headers on.  Any value that does not begin with "on" means off, and nothing is
said about it, so \fB\-headers\fR garbage turns headers off.
.PP
\fB\-debug\fR is the exception.  A value that is neither on nor off is a file
name, the same as it is for the debug command.
.PP
\fB\-quiet\fR is applied before the individual options, so
"\fB\-quiet\fR \fB\-headers\fR on" gives headers on and stats off.
.PP
Every one of these settings is also a command, but a command with no value
means off, not on, so "headers;" turns headers off.  Some settings are
reachable as a command only, cache, opencache and the bind variable commands
among them.
.SH "OUTPUT FORMATS"
.SS plain
Columns are space padded, the column names go above the result set, a line of
= signs goes under the names, and a block of tab indented labels goes under the
rows.  A null is written as the word NULL, which is indistinguishable from the
four character string NULL.  \fB\-headers\fR, \fB\-divider\fR and the
statistics block apply to this format only.
.SS csv
One header row of column names, then one row per row of the result set, and
nothing else.  The header row is always written, whatever \fB\-headers\fR says,
because it is the only way a reader learns what the columns are.
.PP
A field is quoted when it contains a double quote, a comma, a newline, a
carriage return, a tab, or a null byte, and also when it is not a number, or is
a number of 12 or more digits.  An embedded double quote is doubled.  Every
other byte passes through unchanged, so utf\-8 survives.
.PP
A database null is written as an unquoted empty field, and an empty string as a
quoted empty field, that is, as two double quotes.
.SS json
One compact document per command, all on one line, so a run of several commands
is still readable a line at a time.  Wrapped here to fit the page, but written
as one line:
.RS
.nf
{"columns":[{"name":"id","type":"INTEGER"}],"rows":[[1]],
"affectedrows":0,"rowsreturned":1,"fieldsreturned":1,
"elapsed":0.001234}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rows are arrays, since the names are already in the column list.  Best for a
small result set, where the reader wants the whole thing.
.SS jsonl
One json object per line, each typed by a "type" member.  The stats object is
wrapped here too, and is really one line:
.RS
.nf
{"type":"columns","columns":[{"name":"id","type":"INTEGER"}]}
{"type":"row","row":{"id":1}}
{"type":"stats","affectedrows":0,"rowsreturned":1,
"fieldsreturned":1,"elapsed":0.001234}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rows are objects keyed by column name, so a line stands on its own.  Best for a
large result set \- it streams, so head, grep and wc work on it, and a partial
result set is still usable.
.SS "json and jsonl together"
Both carry the column types, which neither plain nor csv does.  A database null
is the json null literal, never "" and never "NULL".  A converted field is
typed, so \fB\-fieldsas\fR number gives a bare json number and \fB\-fieldsas\fR
boolean a bare json true or false.  Everything else, a converted date included,
is a json string.  A value is never truncated.
.PP
The column list is always written and no divider is written, for the same
reason csv always writes its header row.  The statistics are part of the
document and are left out when stats are off, and \fB\-noelapsed\fR drops just
the elapsed member.  A statement with no result set still produces a document,
with an empty column list, an empty row list, and the affected row count in the
statistics.  With \fB\-nextresultset\fR, json gives each result set its own
document and jsonl gives each its own columns, row and stats group.
.PP
The startup banner and the prompt are not written.  An error is one object on
one line on stderr, and stdout gets nothing:
.RS
.nf
{"error":{"number":1,"message":"no such table: nosuchtable"}}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A command that produces a single value writes a one line object keyed by the
command name, so a reader gets the same shape from every one of them:
{"identify":"sqlite"}, {"ping":true}, {"getresultsetbuffersize":100}.
printbinds writes one object holding the three bind lists, and the fields
command writes {"fields":["id","descr"]}.  querytree and translatedquery write
their text as a json string.
.SS "Nulls in every format"
csv, json and jsonl can only tell a null from an empty string while
nullsasnulls is on.  With it off, which is the default, the client library
hands back an empty string for a null and sqlrsh cannot tell the two apart.
.SS "Choosing a format"
A format name is matched ignoring case.  An unrecognized name is an error:
"\fB\-format\fR bogus" exits 1, and "format bogus;" is a failed command.
.SH "FETCHING FIELDS AS A TYPE"
\fB\-fieldsas\fR, and the fieldsas command, choose which getFieldAs method
every field of every result set is fetched with.  There is one setting, shared
by all four formats, since all four are renderings of the same rows.
.PP
A mode only converts a field where the conversion means something, and writes
the field unchanged where it does not, so a mode never turns a name column into
a number, a true or a date.
.TP
raw
The field as the database sent it.  The default.
.TP
number
getFieldAsInteger or getFieldAsDouble, whichever the column type calls for, on
a numeric or bit column.  Every other column passes through.
.TP
boolean
getFieldAsBoolean, on a boolean, bit or numeric column.  Every other column
passes through.  Numeric columns are included because a database with no
boolean type stores a boolean as 0 or 1.  The result is the word true or false,
and a bare json true or false in json and jsonl.
.TP
date
getFieldAsDate, on any column whose value reads as a date.  A field it cannot
read passes through, so no column type test is needed.  The result is
[\-]YYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM:SS[.uuuuuu], carrying only the parts the field had, so a
date column gives a date, a time column a time, and a timestamp both.  A
fraction of a second is written when it is not zero, since a fraction that was
not there and one that was zero read back the same.  A zero month or day, which
is how mysql stores 0000\-00\-00, is not a date, so the whole field passes
through.  The result is a json string, not an object of parts.  The ddmm, yyyyddmm and date
delimiter arguments of getFieldAsDate are not reachable from here; the plain
overload is used.
.PP
getasnumber is an alias: on is fieldsas number and off is fieldsas raw.  When
\fB\-getasnumber\fR and \fB\-fieldsas\fR are both given, \fB\-fieldsas\fR wins.
.PP
A mode name is matched ignoring case, and an unrecognized one is an error:
"\fB\-fieldsas\fR bogus" exits 1, and "fieldsas bogus;" is a failed command.
.SH COMMANDS
Everything in this section is a command rather than a query, and ends with the
delimiter like a query does.  A command that sets something writes nothing when
it works.  A command that fails writes to stderr and, in a script or a
\fB\-command\fR list, stops the run and exits 4.
.SS "Queries, scripts and files"
.TP
reexecute
Runs the previous query again.
.TP
filequery [path] [file]
Runs the whole file as one query.  The path is optional, so one argument is a
file name on its own, and it may be a full path.
.TP
preparefilequery [path] [file]
Prepares the whole file as one query, but does not run it.  reexecute runs it,
and countbindvariables counts what is in it.
.TP
run [file]
Runs the sqlrsh commands in the file.  This is not the same as filequery, which
runs a file as one query.
.TP
@ [file]
Same as run.
.TP
continueonerror on|off
Carries on past a statement that failed, rather than stopping at the first one.
See CONTINUING PAST AN ERROR below.
.TP
help
Writes the command reference.
.TP
exit, quit
Exits.
.SS "Output settings"
.TP
format plain|csv|json|jsonl
Sets the output format.
.TP
headers on|off
The column names above the result set.  plain format only.
.TP
divider on|off
The line of = signs under the column names.  plain format only.
.TP
stats on|off
The statistics after the result set.
.TP
quiet on|off
Shorthand \- quiet on turns headers and stats off, quiet off turns them back
on.
.TP
noelapsed on|off
Leaves the elapsed time out of the statistics.
.TP
fieldsas raw|number|boolean|date
Fetches fields with the matching getFieldAs method, where the column suits it.
raw is the field as the database sent it.  See FETCHING FIELDS AS A TYPE above.
.TP
getasnumber on|off
Fetches numeric fields as numbers.  An alias \- on is fieldsas number and off is
fieldsas raw.
.TP
nullsasnulls on|off
Gets nulls as nulls, rather than as empty strings.
.TP
delimiter [character]
Sets the delimiter, and echoes it back.  delimeter is accepted as a spelling of
it.
.TP
debug on|off
Client library debug messages, to stdout.
.TP
debug [file]
Client library debug messages, to the named file.
.TP
getdebug
Whether debug is on.
.SS "Result sets"
.TP
setresultsetbuffersize [rows]
Fetches rows at a time, rather than all at once.  0 means all at once.  100 to
start with.
.TP
getresultsetbuffersize
The number of rows fetched at a time.
.TP
lazyfetch on|off
Fetches rows as they are asked for, rather than when the query runs.
.TP
nextresultset on|off
Fetches every result set a query returns, rather than just the first.
.TP
columninfo
All of the column metadata of the current result set: name, type, length,
precision, scale, nullable, primarykey, unique, partofkey, unsigned,
zerofilled, binary and autoincrement.  plain writes a block of labels per
column, csv writes a header row and one row per column, and json and jsonl
write one object.
.TP
columninfo on|off
Whether column metadata is fetched at all.  off leaves the names and types
empty.
.TP
columncase mixed|upper|lower
The case of the column names.
.TP
totalrows
The total row count, if the database gives one, and 0 if it does not.
.TP
firstrowindex
The index of the first buffered row.
.TP
endofresultset
Whether the whole result set has arrived.
.TP
suspendresultset
Leaves the result set open for another session to resume.
.TP
resultsetid
The id of the suspended result set.
.TP
resumeresultset [id]
Resumes the result set and writes the rest of it, header, rows and statistics,
in the selected format.  No id means this cursor's own.
.TP
closeresultset
Closes the result set.
.TP
querytree
The parsed query, as xml.
.TP
translatedquery
The query, after translation.
.TP
lastinsertid
The value of the most recently updated auto\-increment or identity column, if
the database has one.
.SS "Caching"
.TP
cache [file] [ttl]
Caches the next result set to the file, with a time\-to\-live of ttl seconds.
600 if left out.
.TP
cacheoff
Stops caching.
.TP
cachefilename
The file the result set is being cached to.
.TP
opencache [file]
Opens a cached result set and writes it.
.TP
resumecachedresultset [id] [file]
Resumes a suspended result set and keeps caching it to the file.  A resume
appends rather than truncating, so the name should be the one the original
session was already caching to.
.PP
A cache file name is taken relative to the cache directory, which lives under
the directory \fB\-localstatedir\fR names.
.SS "Bind variables"
.TP
inputbind
.nf
inputbind [variable] = [value]
inputbind [variable] is null
inputbind [variable] string [length] = [value]
.fi
Defines an input bind variable.  A quoted value is a string, an unquoted one is
an integer or a decimal if it looks like one, and a date if it is
MM/DD/YYYY HH24:MI:SS:uS TZN.  The string form binds exactly length bytes,
cutting off or null padding to fit, which is the only way to spell a value with
an embedded null.
.TP
inputbindblob, inputbindclob
.nf
inputbindblob [variable] = [value]
inputbindblob [variable] is null
inputbindclob [variable] = [value]
inputbindclob [variable] is null
.fi
Defines a blob or clob input bind variable.  A value in quotes has them trimmed
and what is between them unescaped.  An unquoted value is taken as it stands.
.TP
outputbind
.nf
outputbind [variable] string [length]
outputbind [variable] integer
outputbind [variable] double [precision] [scale]
outputbind [variable] date
outputbind [variable] blob
outputbind [variable] clob
outputbind [variable] cursor
.fi
Defines an output bind variable.  A cursor bind has no value of its own;
fetchfrombindcursor is what reads one.
.TP
inputoutputbind
.nf
inputoutputbind [variable] string [length] = [value]
inputoutputbind [variable] integer = [value]
inputoutputbind [variable] double [precision] [scale] = [value]
inputoutputbind [variable] date = [value]
inputoutputbind [variable] [type] ... is null
.fi
Defines an input/output bind variable.
.TP
fetchfrombindcursor [variable]
Writes the result set of a cursor output bind of the query that just ran, the
way a query writes one.  Needs a database with ref cursors, so Oracle or DB2.
.TP
printbinds
All three bind variable lists.
.TP
printinputbind, printoutputbind, printinputoutputbind
One of the three bind variable lists.
.TP
clearinputbind, clearoutputbind, clearinputoutputbind
.nf
clearinputbind [variable]
clearoutputbind [variable]
clearinputoutputbind [variable]
.fi
Clears one bind variable out of one of the three lists.  With no variable name,
clears the whole list.  A variable name that is not in the list is an error,
and the command fails.
.TP
clearbinds
Clears all three lists.
.TP
countbindvariables
The number of bind variables in the query that was prepared last.  Every way of
running a query except preparefilequery also executes it, so preparefilequery
is what makes this useful.
.TP
validatebinds on|off
Ignores bind variables the query does not have, rather than failing.  No value
means on.  The shell keeps the setting and applies it to every query, because
preparing a query clears the flag on the cursor.
.TP
validbind [variable]
Whether the variable really was a bind variable of the query that just ran.
Only means anything after a query that ran with validatebinds on.
.TP
bindformat
The bind variable format of the database.
.TP
bindvariabledelimiters
The bind variable delimiters in use.
.TP
bindvariabledelimiters [delimiters]
Sets them, from the set ? : @ $.
.TP
bindvariabledelimitersupported [delimiter]
Whether one of ? : @ $ is supported.
.SS "Substitutions"
.TP
substitution [variable] = [value]
Replaces $(variable) in the query text when the query is prepared.  A
substitution is not a bind variable.  The value is typed the way an input bind
value is, except that there is no date and no null.
.TP
clearsubstitutions
Clears the substitution list.
.SS "Session and database information"
.TP
ping
Pings the database.
.TP
identify
The type of database.
.TP
dbversion
The version of the database.
.TP
dbhostname
The host name of the database.
.TP
dbipaddress
The ip address of the database.
.TP
clientversion
The version of the client library.
.TP
serverversion
The version of the server.
.TP
databasefeature [feature]
Whether the database has the feature.
.TP
nextvalformat
The sequence next\-value format of the database.
.TP
setclientinfo [info]
Sets the client info string.
.TP
getclientinfo
The client info string.
.TP
endsession
Ends the session.  The next command starts a new one.
.TP
final on|off
One session per query.
.TP
suspendsession
Leaves the session open for another client to resume.
.TP
connectionport
The port of the suspended session.
.TP
connectionsocket
The unix socket of the suspended session.  Both of these only mean anything
after a suspendsession.
.TP
resumesession [port] [socket]
Resumes a suspended session.  An inet session has no socket, so the socket is
optional.
.TP
connect timeout [seconds[.fraction]]
The timeout for the next connect, which is what a resumesession or a reconnect
after an endsession uses.  It does not apply to the session sqlrsh is already
in.
.TP
getconnecttimeout
The connect timeout, or \-1 when it is off.
.TP
response timeout [seconds[.fraction]]
The timeout for a response from the server.
.TP
getresponsetimeout
The response timeout, or \-1 when it is off.
.PP
A timeout is decimal seconds, so 5.25 sets five and a quarter seconds and 0.5
sets half a second.  Leave the dot out for a whole number of seconds.  The
smallest unit is the microsecond, so a seventh digit past the dot only rounds
the sixth.  Both the setters and the get commands report a timeout in the same
form, with the fraction padded to 6 places - half a second comes back as
0.500000 - so what they print can be given straight back to the setter
unchanged.
.PP
The SQLR_CLIENT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT and SQLR_CLIENT_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT environment
variables set the same two timeouts, in the same form, for any program that
links the client library, including sqlrsh itself.  A command given at the
sqlrsh prompt overrides what they set.
.SS "Databases, catalogs and schemas"
.TP
use [database]
Changes the current database or schema.
.TP
currentdb
The current database or schema.
.TP
usecatalog [catalog]
Changes the current catalog.
.TP
currentcatalog
The current catalog.
.TP
useschema [schema]
Changes the current schema.
.TP
currentschema
The current schema.
.TP
currentuser
The current user.
.TP
databaseisschema
Whether the database is what other databases would call a schema.
.SS "Transactions"
.TP
autocommit on|off
Commits after every query.
.TP
getautocommit
Whether autocommit is on.
.TP
begin, commit, rollback
Begins a transaction, commits, or rolls back.
.TP
txqueries on|off
Sends begin, commit and rollback to the database as queries, rather than
running them as commands.
.TP
intransaction
Whether a transaction is open.
.TP
isolationlevel
The current isolation level.
.TP
isolationlevel [level]
Sets it.
.TP
defaultisolationlevel
The isolation level the session starts with.
.TP
transactionmodel
The current transaction model.
.TP
transactionmodel [model]
Sets it.
.TP
defaulttransactionmodel
The transaction model the session starts with.
.SS "Metadata"
.TP
show databases [like 'pattern']
A list of the known databases or schemas.
.TP
show catalogs [like 'pattern']
A list of the known catalogs.
.TP
show schemas [like 'pattern']
A list of the known schemas.
.TP
show tables [like 'pattern']
A list of the known tables, views, aliases and synonyms.
.TP
show only
.nf
show only tables [like 'pattern']
show only views [like 'pattern']
show only aliases [like 'pattern']
show only synonyms [like 'pattern']
.fi
A list of one kind of object only.
.TP
show table types
A list of the table types the database has.
.TP
show columns in [table] [like 'pattern']
Column metadata for the table.
.TP
describe [table]
The same column metadata that show columns writes.
.TP
fields [table]
The column names of the table, on one line.
.TP
show primary keys in [table] [like 'pattern']
The primary keys of the table.
.TP
show keys and indexes in [table] [like 'pattern']
The keys and indexes of the table.
.TP
show procedures [like 'pattern']
A list of the known procedures.
.TP
show procedure parameters in [procedure] [like 'pattern']
The parameters of the procedure.
.TP
show type info [for type]
The types the database supports.  All of them without a for clause.
.TP
show lastinsertid
The last insert id, as a result set.
.PP
A pattern goes in single quotes, and a single quote inside one is doubled.  The
wildcards are the database's own, usually % and _.
.PP
databases, catalogs, schemas, tables, table types, columns, procedures, type
info and the show only forms each take an optional list format, right after the
category:
.RS
.nf
show tables mysql
show tables odbc like 'a%'
show columns jdbc in mytable
show only views odbc
.fi
.RE
.PP
The list format decides the columns of the result set: mysql gives what a MySQL
client would see, odbc what an ODBC client would see, and jdbc what a JDBC
client would see.  Left out, the columns are whatever the database itself
returns.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
0
Success.  A genuinely interactive session always exits 0, even if a query at
the prompt failed \- it isn't a batch.  \fB\-batch\fR is, so a failed
statement there is a 4, the same as \fB\-script\fR and \fB\-command\fR.
.TP
1
Usage error.  A run with no \fB\-id\fR, no \fB\-host\fR and no \fB\-socket\fR,
an option that takes a value given without one, an unrecognized \fB\-format\fR
or \fB\-fieldsas\fR name, a \fB\-locale\fR value that setlocale rejects, or an
\fB\-id\fR whose configured password encryption is missing or one-way.
.TP
2
The file named by \fB\-script\fR could not be opened.  A file that the run
command could not open is a failed command in the outer script, so that is a 4,
not a 2.
.TP
3
Connection or authentication failure.  The connection is checked at startup, so
this is reported before any command runs, in every mode.
.TP
4
At least one statement in a script, a \fB\-command\fR list, a \fB\-query\fR,
or a \fB\-batch\fR run failed, not necessarily all of them.  Without
\fB\-continueonerror\fR the run stopped at that statement, and with it the
run went on.
.SH "CONTINUING PAST AN ERROR"
\fB\-continueonerror\fR, and the continueonerror command, make a script, a
\fB\-command\fR list, or a \fB\-batch\fR run go through every statement rather
than stopping at the first one that failed.
.PP
A statement rejected before it reached the database, such as a bad
\fBformat\fR name or a \fBrun\fR of a file that could not be opened, and a
statement the database rejected count the same.  Each failed statement still
writes its own error.
.PP
A failure that takes the session down with it stops the run anyway, because the
rest of the statements have nothing left to run against.
.PP
At the end of a run with anything failed, the number of failed statements is
written to stderr as "statements failed: N", and the exit code is 4.  With
continueonerror off the run stopped at its first failure, so the count would
always be 1 and none is written.
.PP
A failing command in ~/.sqlrshrc is not counted and does not change the exit
code.
.SH FILES
.TP
~/.sqlrshrc
Commands run at startup, in every mode, before the script or the command.  A
missing file is not an error, and a failing command in it does not change the
exit status.
.TP
~/.sqlrsh_history
The last 100 lines typed at the prompt.
.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive session with server at svr:9000 as usr/pwd.
.IP
sqlrsh \-host svr \-port 9000 \-user usr \-password pwd
.PP
Interactive session with local server on socket /tmp/svr.sock as usr/pwd.
.IP
sqlrsh \-socket /tmp/svr.sock \-user usr \-password pwd
.PP
Interactive session using connection info and credentials from an instance
defined in the default configuration.
.IP
sqlrsh \-id myinst
.PP
Interactive session using connection info and credentials from an instance
defined in the config file ./myconfig.conf
.IP
sqlrsh \-config ./myconfig.conf \-id myinst
.PP
Non\-interactive session, running commands from ./script.sql
.IP
sqlrsh \-id myinst \-script ./script.sql
.PP
Non\-interactive session, running query "select * from mytable" with csv
output.
.IP
sqlrsh \-id myinst \-command "select * from mytable" \-quiet \-format csv
.PP
Non\-interactive session, piping commands into stdin, with no banner or
prompts mixed into the output.
.IP
echo "select 1;" | sqlrsh \-id myinst \-batch
.br
sqlrsh \-id myinst \-batch < script.sql
.PP
Non\-interactive session, one json document per command, on stdout, with errors
on stderr.  nullsasnulls is what makes a null come out as the json null
literal.
.sp
.RS
.nf
sqlrsh \-id myinst \-format json \e
        \-command "nullsasnulls on; select * from mytable"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non\-interactive session, one jsonl object per line, piped to a reader that
takes a line at a time.
.sp
.RS
.nf
sqlrsh \-id myinst \-format jsonl \e
        \-command "select * from mybigtable" | myreader
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non\-interactive session, checking the exit status.
.IP
sqlrsh \-id myinst \-script ./script.sql || echo "failed with $?"
